        "id": "cs314-2014-spring-final-q6",
        "title": "6. data structures",
        "points": 15,
        "type": "code",
        "language": "java",
        "prompt": "Write the dequeue method for a priority queue that uses a binary search tree as the internal storage container. The front element of the queue is the smallest element based on the Comparable interface.",
        "reference": "public class TreePriorityQueue<E extends Comparable<? super E>> {\n\n       private BSTNode<E> root; // root == null if tree empty\n\n       // no other instance variables\n\n       private static class BSTNode<E extends Comparable<? super E>> {\n            // recall you can access these directly from the\n            // TreePriorityQueue class\n            private E data;\n            private BSTNode<E> left;\n            private BSTNode<E> right;\n       }\n\nImportant: You may not use recursion in your solution.\nYou may not use any other Java classes or methods besides the BSTNode class.\n\nThere are no precoditions to the method. If the priority queue is empty, then the method returns null.\n\nComplete the instance method for the TreePriorityQueue class on the next page.\n\n/* pre: none\n   post: remove and return the front (smallest) element in this\n          priority queue.\n         If the priority queue is empty this method returns null.\n*/\npublic E dequeue {",
        "stub": "// Write your Java solution here.\n",
        "answer": "public E deqeue() {\n    E result = null;\n    if(root != null) {\n        if(root.left == null) {\n            result = root.data;\n            root = root.right;\n        }\n        else {\n            BSTNode<E> temp = root;\n            while(temp.left.left != null) {\n                temp = temp.left;\n            }\n            result = temp.left.data;\n            temp.left = temp.left.right;\n        }\n    }\n    return result;\n}",
