        "type": "code",
        "language": "java",
        "prompt": "To demonstrate encapsulation and the syntax for building a class in Java, we developed a GenericList class that can store elements of any data type. Recall our GenericList class stores the elements of the list in the first N elements of a native array. An element's position in the list is the same as the element's position in the array. The array may be larger than the list it represents. Complete a method that removes the first n values from the calling list.",
        "reference": "-> indicates the calling list after the method has completed.\n\nExamples:\n[A, B, C, D, A].removeFirstN(1) -> [B, C, D, A]\n[A, B, C, D, A].removeFirstN(2) -> [C, D, A]\n[A, B, C, D, A].removeFirstN(5) -> []\n\nThe GenericList class:\n\npublic class GenericList<E> {\n\n       private E[] con;\n       private int size;\n\nYou may not use any methods from the GenericList class unless you implement them yourself as\na part of your solution.\n\nComplete the method on the next page.\n\n// pre: 0 < n <= size()\n// post: The first n elements of this list are removed. Remaining elements are shifted to the front of the list. size() = old size() - n",
        "stub": "// pre: 0 < n <= size()\n// post: Per the problem description.\npublic void removeFirstN(int n) {\n\n}",
        "answer": "public void removeFirstN(int n) {\n    final int NUM_SHIFT = size - n;\n    // shift elements down\n    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_SHIFT; i++) {\n        con[i] = con[i + n];\n    }\n    // null out empty spots;\n    for (int i = NUM_SHIFT; i < size; i++) {\n        con[i] = null;\n    }\n    size -= n;\n}",
